1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,698
This man hijacked a passenger jet, collected $200,000, and jumped into the night.

2
00:00:06,798 --> 00:00:08,567
He was never seen again.

3
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:12,790
But what's most unsettling isn't what he did. It's how calm he was.

4
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:22,108
Portland, 1971. A man calling himself Dan Cooper boards a flight to Seattle.

5
00:00:22,208 --> 00:00:26,435
He orders a bourbon and soda, then hands the flight attendant a note.

6
00:00:26,535 --> 00:00:29,008
She thinks it's his phone number. It isn't.

7
00:00:29,108 --> 00:00:31,856
Miss, you'd better look at that note. I have a bomb.

8
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,463
Now, notice what he doesn't do.

9
00:00:34,563 --> 00:00:40,598
He doesn't shout. He doesn't threaten anyone. Most passengers never realize they're being hijacked.

10
00:00:40,698 --> 00:00:44,636
He asks for $200,000, and four parachutes.

11
00:00:44,736 --> 00:00:51,328
Four. Extra parachutes imply he might take a hostage, so no one can risk giving him a sabotaged one.

12
00:00:51,428 --> 00:00:53,280
This isn't panic. It's a plan.

13
00:00:53,933 --> 00:00:58,857
In Seattle, he trades the passengers for the cash, and gives the crew his orders.

14
00:00:58,957 --> 00:01:03,924
Fly to Mexico. Low and slow. Landing gear down. Cabin unpressurized.

15
00:01:04,024 --> 00:01:07,814
He knows this plane has a rear staircase that can open in flight.

16
00:01:08,467 --> 00:01:15,119
At 8:13 p.m., over the forests of southwest Washington, the tail of the plane jolts upward.

17
00:01:15,219 --> 00:01:17,169
When it lands in Reno, he's gone.

18
00:01:17,933 --> 00:01:23,976
The FBI first assumed he was an expert skydiver. Later, they concluded the opposite.

19
00:01:24,076 --> 00:01:31,206
As one agent put it, no experienced parachutist would jump at night, in the rain, wearing loafers and a trench coat.

20
00:01:31,306 --> 00:01:36,256
A man who planned every detail of the hijacking, and almost nothing about the landing.

21
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:43,702
In 1980, a boy found $5,800 of the ransom on the bank of the Columbia River.

22
00:01:43,802 --> 00:01:46,449
The rest was never found. Neither was he.

23
00:01:46,549 --> 00:01:51,243
It's still the only unsolved hijacking in American aviation history.

24
00:01:51,343 --> 00:01:57,598
Maybe he died that night in the woods. Or maybe the calmest man on that plane got exactly what he planned.

